WHAT THIS ESTABLISHES

The crossing reproduces on a chain built independently of `101`'s, which is
a second instance of the phenomenon rather than a rerun of one model.

And the crossing is not a two-way fork. At every swept k the best arm is a
fine-grid arm with a truncate-then-round switch depth, and the switch depth
moves with k. Reading the crossing as `pick A below the crossing and B above`
takes the pointwise minimum of two arms when a third is available that is at
least as good at every k and strictly better at most of them.

This is I13's shape exactly: not one rule over the category, but an arm whose
const predicate is the chain depth, applying on its region and nowhere else.
It is also the reason the region-versus-coordinate question is load-bearing
rather than bookkeeping. Put chain depth in the cost vector and the table
averages over it and this arm cannot be expressed at all. Put it in the region
and the arm falls out. What it costs is that the depth has to be const, which
p4 tests.
